---
/**
 * Ryan's own receipts.
 *
 * Three reviewers asked for this independently, and the sharpest version was: "The site
 * shows Ryan's story, not his receipts. Where is Ryan's own REAL RICH RECEIPTS folder?"
 *
 * The point is not content. Every chapter of the book asks the reader to keep proof of what
 * they built, and the author had none on display — which is the one thing a competitor
 * cannot fake and the one thing this project cannot argue its way out of. A book about proof
 * over performance whose author shows no proof is performing.
 *
 * WHY MOST OF THIS PAGE IS EMPTY, AND WHY THAT IS THE CORRECT STATE. Ryan has not supplied
 * the material yet. Nothing on this site is allowed to write in his voice or invent his
 * work — the same rule that keeps Chapter 16 saying "not written yet" instead of being
 * filled with something plausible. A named empty slot is honest. A filled fake one is the
 * exact failure /hill exists to warn about, committed by us, on the page whose whole subject
 * is evidence.
 *
 * So this page ships with the slots named and the gaps stated, in the same register /get
 * uses for "Retailer link pending" and /accessibility uses for its six unverified items.
 * When the material arrives it drops into slots that already say what they are for.
 */
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import ChapterFurniture from "../../components/ChapterFurniture.astro";

/** Each slot names what goes there and what is honest to say while it is empty. */
const SLOTS = [
  {
    what: "His first website",
    why: "Not this one. The first one, before he knew what he was doing.",
    state: "Ryan has not sent this yet.",
  },
  {
    what: "The first thing he built with an AI coding tool, mistakes still in it",
    why: "The mistakes are the point. If you only show the parts that worked, that is a highlight reel, not a receipt.",
    state: "Ryan has not sent this yet.",
    href: "/how-this-was-made",
    hrefLabel: "What an AI coding tool is, and what it got wrong here",
  },
  {
    what: "A photograph of the printed build receipts in his folder",
    why: "The book tells you to start a real folder. Either Ryan has one too, or that instruction is just words.",
    state: "Ryan has not sent this yet.",
  },
  {
    what: "His own Real Rich Scoreboard, filled in",
    why: "Six areas, scored honestly — including the ones he would rather not show you.",
    state: "Ryan has not sent this yet.",
    href: "/worksheet/real-rich-scoreboard",
    hrefLabel: "The scoreboard",
  },
  {
    what: "The karting results, as receipts rather than as a story",
    why: "This is the one that is already backed up. The results are published in independent motorsport press, and every claim on it carries its source.",
    state: "Sourced",
    href: "/proof",
    hrefLabel: "See the author page",
    /* The one slot that is not empty. It used to be inferred from `href`, which broke the
       moment an empty slot needed a link of its own — an unsent receipt was rendering in
       the "this one is done" weight. State is stated, never derived. */
    filled: true,
  },
];
---

<Page
  title="Ryan's own receipts — the author's folder, mostly empty"
  description="The book asks every reader to keep proof of what they built. This is where the author's own proof goes. Most of it is not here yet, and that is stated rather than filled in."
>
  <ChapterFurniture
    eyebrow="Show your working"
    title="Ryan's own receipts"
    subtitle="The book asks you to keep proof. This is the author's folder, and most of it is empty."
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        Build 01 of the book tells you to start a folder called REAL RICH RECEIPTS and put
        every piece of proof inside it. It would be strange to ask that and then show
        nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this page exists, and most of it is empty. That is not a gap we plan to quietly
        fill in later — it is the honest state, written down. Nothing on this site is
        allowed to invent Ryan's work or write in his voice, which is the same reason{" "}
        <a href="/c/16">Chapter 16</a> still says it is not written yet instead of being
        filled with something that sounds about right.
      </p>
      <p>
        An empty slot with a name on it tells you exactly what is missing. A full one that
        was made up tells you nothing, and it would be exactly the thing{" "}
        <a href="/hill">the Napoleon Hill audit</a> warns about, done by us, on the page
        about proof.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="folder">
    <h2 id="folder">What goes in the folder</h2>
    <dl class="slots">
      {
        SLOTS.map((s) => (
          <div class="slot">
            <dt class="slot__what">
              {s.what}
              <span class="slot__state" data-filled={"filled" in s && s.filled ? "yes" : "no"}>
                {s.state}
              </span>
            </dt>
            <dd class="slot__why">
              {s.why}
              {/* The link is a sentence of its own, with its own verb. It used to be the
                  bare label plus a concatenated full stop, which rendered as an orphaned
                  noun phrase punctuated as a sentence: "The author page." */}
              {s.href && (
                <>
                  {" "}
                  <a href={s.href}>{s.hrefLabel}</a>.
                </>
              )}
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="yours">
    <h2 id="yours">Yours is the one that matters</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Ryan's folder is running late. Yours does not have to be.{" "}
        <a href="/receipts">Your own receipts</a> are kept in this browser, and they print as
        the folder index that goes in the front of your REAL RICH RECEIPTS folder — printing
        them is the point, because a browser can clear itself and paper cannot.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-inline-size: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 3rem);
  }

  .prose {
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
  }

  .prose p + p {
    margin-block-start: 1rem;
  }

  /* This page carried class="lede" with no rule behind it, so the first sentence a reader
     sees rendered at body size and sat flush against the bottom edge of the header. Same
     rule as /proof, which is the page this one is a wing of. */
  .lede {
    margin-block-start: 1.75rem;
    font-size: var(--text-subtitle);
    line-height: var(--text-subtitle--line-height);
    color: var(--text-strong);
  }

  .section {
    margin-block-start: 3rem;
  }

  .slots {
    margin-block-start: 1.5rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  .slot {
    border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--rule-hairline);
    padding-inline-start: 1rem;
  }

  .slot__what {
    color: var(--text-strong);
    font-weight: 700;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
  }

  /* The state label is deliberately quiet, and deliberately NOT a flag. Green here would
     read as the traffic-light system, which is about whether a decision needs an adult —
     nothing to do with whether Ryan has sent a photograph yet. Weight carries the
     difference instead of colour, so it also survives being printed in black and white. */
  .slot__state {
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: var(--text-label--letter-spacing);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .slot__state[data-filled="yes"] {
    color: var(--text-strong);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .slot__why {
    margin-block-start: 0.35rem;
    max-inline-size: var(--container-measure);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Printed, this is a folder index: someone ticks the empty slots off by hand as the
     material arrives. The rules stay, the muted greys go to full contrast. */
  @media print {
    .slot__why,
    .slot__state {
      color: inherit;
    }

    /* A slot is one item in that index. Split across a page break, its title and its
       "not sent yet" line print on one sheet and the reason on the next, with the spine
       rule running off the bottom of the page. Same protection global.css gives .box. */
    .slot {
      break-inside: avoid;
    }
  }
</style>
